  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAs high pressure remains dominant over the area, the majority of the \narea should remain dry through Saturday. The exception to this may \nbe across counties east of I-77 on Saturday as increased moisture \npushes north over an area of weak isentropic lift. Any showers that \ndo develop should remain light and short lived given weak forcing \nand the thunder potential remains minimal. Highs on Saturday will be \na bit warmer, climbing into the low to mid 80s.\n\nDuring the overnight period tonight and Saturday night, patchy fog \nis possible especially south of US-30 where low level moisture will \nincrease under an inversion. Confidence in the extent or density \nremains low due to some lingering clouds possible tonight. Overnight \nlows will drop into the upper 50s to mid 60s both nights. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nBeginning on Sunday, the dominant upper level ridge will begin to \nsink south across the Southern Plains which will allow a trough to \ndig over Great Lakes region. This upper level trough in conjunction \nwith multiple mid-level shortwaves will provide synoptic energy to \nsupport periods of rainfall and thunderstorms through the middle of \nnext week. Initially on Sunday, scattered showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible especially in the afternoon with the aide of \ndaytime instability, but overall impacts should remain minimal. The \ngreatest potential for severe weather will be on Monday as a \nmoistening deep layer ahead of a cold front will push north across \nthe area as a mid-level shortwave pushes east. Increased moisture \ncoupled with daytime heating should result in adequate instability \nacross the entire area. In addition, by Monday the area will be \nsituated under the left exit region of the upper level jet, further \nenhancing lift and increasing deep layer sheer. These conditions \nshould result in clusters of thunderstorms developing with some of \nthem reaching strong to severe thresholds. There remains some \nuncertainty in the forecast this far out, especially with how any \nSunday night precipitation may impact the forecast on Monday. If \nshowers and clouds linger into Monday morning, there will likely be \na diminished threat a bit given reduced instability. will have to \nclosely monitor the evolution of model guidance over the next few \ndays to get a better handle on storm potential Monday, but for now \nSPC has issued a Day 4 SWO with the entire area highlighted in\n15%.",
